A||N||13||Eric_PA||3800 rebuild finished with some pics||||10||
Z||000000||Eric_PA||09-03-2001||09:44 AM||||It's about time for me. I finally finished a semi performance rebuild on a 1990 3800 Series I engine. It cost more than to just purchase a Series II supercharged. I guess I may have went the wrong route, but I'll finally see once it's in.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Aug22$10.jpg">  <P>Pic of the heads... 5 angle valve grind and some bowl work   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Aug22$05.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Aug25$04.jpg">  <P>All ready to put into the cradle. I'm waiting to get the sub frame back from being sand blasted. The cradle was pulled from a salvage yard with the help of Rockcrawl.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep01$12.jpg">  <P>Here's a pic of Chris West's engine mount. I had to drill and tap a hole in the block to use it. The hole is the top left one. I was scared to do it, but now that it's done, no sweat  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep01$05.jpg">   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||HummelHund||09-03-2001||10:42 AM||||Is that the lucky Fiero's nose poking out behind the finished engine in the fourth picture? Very nice looking engine, wish I had the resources to do rebuilds.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>84' SE 2M4 WS6 (Dad's Project)||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||09-03-2001||11:18 AM||||Lookin good Eric. I hope you get that thing finished before the snow falls so I can whoop your ass with the 4.5!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> No nitrous allowed! Hey, that must be Spring's new '88 coupe in the backround. I bet she's happy.||||reg||7
Z||000003||Joe Torma||09-03-2001||11:27 AM||||Looks good...I'm sure you'll be happy with it!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm||||reg||10
Z||000004||pontiacs4me||09-03-2001||08:54 PM||||What all did you do to it to spend so much money?  What cam did you use?  How much power do you expect from it when its all said and done?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric_PA||09-03-2001||09:55 PM||||That white 88 coupe in the back now belongs to my girlfriend. She picked it up this week and that thing is nice. I would like to drop the 3800 in it if it where mine. As far as the cam I am using, it is stock but from a newer Series II 3800. I forget the year off hand, I had the machine shop find somthing better than it came with. The only other option was a custom grind, and for the money was not an option. I finished a few things up on it tonight and got the beretta trans on. I was undecided with what trans I was going to use  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Well here is another pic... the camera batteries where going dead so it's a bit grainy.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep03$04.jpg"> <P>I forgot to mention that this engine is going to be put into my black AUTOMATIC 86 fastback. So I not only am wrestling with the install, but also converting over to a 5 speed. I hope to see a difference from a 150K 2.8 auto to a stick 3800. As far as HP goes, I hope to see 180HP to 225HP. It's no race engine, but should be a bit pepier than stock. Once it is broken in I will get the car on a dyno. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Springtyme||09-04-2001||09:26 AM||||He wouldn't trust me enough to put that into my car, I'd probably crack it up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Eventually I want to convert mine to a 5 speed, and make it a 6 cyl, but I want to wait until I have fun with it now so as it is. He won't admit it, but I kicked his 86 GT's a** going down the highway... At least I get one victory before the new engine goes in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Springtyme.jpg"> <P>            ~Springtyme~||||reg||7
Z||000007||88formula||09-04-2001||04:49 PM||||Take the sharp edges off those combustion chambers! <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Chris Schaefer||09-04-2001||08:13 PM||||Hey Eric,<P>Congrats on getting near the end of your project.  The idea of using a series I 3800 is not bad.  You can really get good power out of one of those motors when the right parts are used.<P>Enjoy! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||NOS3800||09-04-2001||11:17 PM||||How much did it cost to get the heads ported and polished? Was there a valve job done? I am curious cause I am thinking of getting mine done on my Series II motor. One of the GrandPrix specialties shops sells them for 800 for the pair and they are maxed out with a valve job and everything to gain an extra 23 HP according to there dyno.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric_PA||09-05-2001||07:12 AM||||The engine is now in the subframe after a little grinding and cutting  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> IT looks so good in there. I will have a pic of it tonight to post on here. Thanks for all the compliments, I am so excited about this project because it is finally becoming a reality. I did find that a few things have to be changed. I thought I wasn't going to need an idler pulley when omitting the power steering pump, but I was wrong, and I have the wrong coil pack and AC compressor. It looks like another trip to the pick and pull yard, sorry Spring got to do it. <P>88Formula, I was going to match all the ports to the gaskets, but the machinist that I have been consulting insisted that it not be done and only a bowl and valve job with heavier springs would be necessary with these heads. I did clean up the exhaust manifolds a bit with a die grinder, there was a lot of metal to remove there. I'm glad there are carbide bits  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P>NOS3800, with the 5 angle valve job, bowl work, new springs, valve guide seals, and pocket porting, the cost was somewhere around 350. I should have gone all out on these heads, but for the money/peformance it wasn't worth it for me to do anymore, parts for this engine where more expensive than usual. For instance the rings alone where over $200 for the set. They where chromoly rings, but still a lot. I will post another pic of this tonight.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric_PA||09-05-2001||07:00 PM||||Here is a current pic. I just recently found out that a hole saw works very well to remove the bushings. It beats the crap out of the drill, but they come out real fast.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sep05$04.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||09-05-2001||07:36 PM||||I meant the sharp edges around the combustion chambers or where the flat surface (quench) meets the combustion chamber. If you had the heads milled or even if you didn’t this sharp edge can get red hot during the combustion cycle and cause detonation. Gasket matching the intakes usually doesn’t do much but blending the bowls does. <P>That motor looks mighty familiar! I had a Bonniville with that exact same motor (165 horsepower 205-ft. lbs. of torque) and it lasted forever.    <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric_PA||09-06-2001||06:51 AM||||88Formula... ok now I know what you meant. I remember reading about that and it wasn't done to the heads. It's too late now for me to get in there. I wish I would have been posting the progress instead of being too lazy to get the pics on the computer. I even remember running my fingers over that edge thinking to myself that maybe I should take down the edge. Darn, I hope it isn't that critical. Thanks for mentioning something, it sure will be in my mind for the next time I do something like this.||||reg||11
